    "lawSection": "Tourism",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S3807\n\nSPONSOR: THOMAS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act creating the \"Empire state cricket board\" that\nwill promote and grow the game of cricket\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo promote the game of cricket in New York.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nEstablishes state-wide cricket board that will oversee and govern the\npromotion and growth of the game in the state.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe game of cricket was first played in England in the 14th century, and\nby the 18th century, cricket became a national phenomenon. The first\nwritten rules of the game were adopted in 1744.\n\nThe first report of a cricket match in North America was in 1751, when\nthe New York Gazette reported a match between teams from London and New\nYork City. In 1844, the United States and Canada faced off in a cricket\ngame that was the first international sporting event in the modern\nworld.\n\nCricket has come a long way since the 19th century. Today, billions of\npeople that live across continents and regions ranging from the Caribbe-\nan, to Europe, to Africa and South Asia play cricket. In North America\ncricket is gaining popularity, thanks to the huge influx of immigrants\nfrom these regions. In 2005, New York City mayor Michael Bloomberg\nannounced a plan to build a $1.5 million cricket pitch in St. Albans\nPark, Queens. Today, there are parks across New York where cricket is\nplayed.\n\nNew York state takes pride in its diversity, and New York City is the\nworld's most cosmopolitan city. Both of which makes for an ideal place\nfor a game such as cricket to thrive. There is a great deal of support\nand enthusiasm for the game amongst New Yorkers who seek to expand the\ngame, whiehes hand in hand with the internationalism of the state. New\nYork serves as a cultural hub for many regions of the world, cricket\nwill be yet another addition to that multicultural New York.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the state.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
